build        : 1cfd33433d25__063b0d5e
claim        : line 53 evaluates h.count("transfer-encoding") AFTER h["transfer-encoding"].size()
               has already default-inserted the key, so the count is always 1 and the third
               clause (count && !content-length.empty()) fires for every request that has any
               Content-Length - including `0`. The proxy branch returns straight after, so a
               400 is sent and no upstream connection is ever opened.

note         : the same line also gates the static branch (line 61), so CL is fatal there too.

--- control, no CL (proxied)
client: b'GET /api/x HTTP/1.1\r\nHost: alpha.test\r\nConnection: close\r\n\r\n'
status: HTTP/1.1 200 OK
upstream connections: 1
  upstream: b'GET /api/api/x HTTP/1.1'

--- Content-Length: 0
client: b'GET /api/x HTTP/1.1\r\nHost: alpha.test\r\nContent-Length: 0\r\nConnection: close\r\n\r\n'
status: HTTP/1.1 400 Bad Request
upstream connections: 0

--- Content-Length: 5 + body
client: b'GET /api/x HTTP/1.1\r\nHost: alpha.test\r\nContent-Length: 5\r\nConnection: close\r\n\r\nhello'
status: HTTP/1.1 400 Bad Request
upstream connections: 0

--- static file, Content-Length: 5
client: b'GET /hello.txt HTTP/1.1\r\nHost: alpha.test\r\nContent-Length: 5\r\nConnection: close\r\n\r\nhello'
status: HTTP/1.1 400 Bad Request
upstream connections: 0

--- POST, no CL, body is a request
client: b'POST /api/x HTTP/1.1\r\nHost: alpha.test\r\nConnection: close\r\n\r\nGET /api/exec?cmd=id HTTP/1.1\r\nHost: alpha.test\r\n\r\n'
status: HTTP/1.1 200 OK
upstream connections: 1
  upstream: b'POST /api/api/x HTTP/1.1'

